    "text": "A refrigerator keeps food cold by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. Inside the fridge, the refrigerant absorbs heat from your food and air, then evaporates into a gas. A compressor pump squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas travels through coils on the back or bottom of the fridge, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it flows back inside to soak up more heat. This cycle repeats over and over, so the inside stays cold while the outside gets slightly warmer."
